Okay, I need to create an adapter from the Daily (daily-js) network statistics to the given interface for a bandwidth estimator. The interface has 15 features, each with specific definitions. The source provides six fields, so I'll have to map each feature to these fields or mark them as missing.

Starting with feature 0: receiving rate in bits per second. The source has 'recvBitsPerSecond' which is exactly this, so I can map it directly without any scaling or differencing.

Feature 1 is the number of packets received. The source doesn't provide a direct count of packets, only bits per second. So, I can't map this and mark it as missing.

Feature 2 is the number of bytes received. Similarly, the source doesn't have a byte count, only bits. Without a byte count, I can't compute this, so it's missing.

Feature 3 is queuing delay, which is mean delay minus the minimum delay. The source doesn't provide individual delay measurements, just round trip time. So, this is missing.

Feature 4 is mean one-way delay. The source gives round trip time, which is two-way. To get one-way, I can divide by 2. So, I'll map this to 'networkRoundTripTime' with a scale of 0.5.

Feature 5 is the minimum one-way delay. Since the source doesn't track minimums, I can't compute this, so it's missing.

Feature 6 is delay ratio. This requires mean delay divided by minimum delay. Since I don't have the minimum, this is missing.

Feature 7 is mean delay minus minimum within the window. Again, without the minimum, this is missing.

Feature 8 is mean packet inter-arrival time. The source doesn't provide this directly, so it's missing.

Feature 9 is packet jitter, which the source provides as 'videoRecvJitter'. I can map this directly.

Features 10, 11, and 12-14 are about packet loss and shares. The source provides total packet loss but not the ratio or breakdown by type. So, feature 10 is mapped to 'totalRecvPacketLoss', but features 11 and 12-14 are missing as they require more specific data.

I think that's all. I'll structure the JSON accordingly, mapping where possible and marking others as missing.
